This session made me think about AI Product Management more from the perspective of making the right product decision, rather than just building something with AI.
One point that stood out to me was the idea of having an AI opportunity thesis before jumping into development.
We should start with assumptions and then collect evidence to see whether those assumptions are actually true.
I liked this because it prevents us from forcing AI into a problem just because AI is currently popular.
Sometimes a simpler solution may solve the customer problem better, and as a PM we should be honest about that.
That was probably one of the most practical lessons for me.

Adding AI to an existing product is different from designing a product around AI from the beginning.
Another thing I found interesting was how AI has reduced the cost of prototyping and failure.

Another takeaway for me was that a PM should understand enough about the domain and technology to lead the team effectively.

I also liked the homework of looking at real PM and AI PM job descriptions because it connects classroom concepts with what companies actually expect.
It made me realise that the role is becoming more focused on outcomes, problem ownership and decision-making.
Overall, this session helped me see that an AI PM should not be an “AI promoter” but someone who can make a balanced product decision based on evidence.
